## Summary
`/api/launches` forwards the raw upstream Launch Library 2 error response body verbatim to the client when no stale cache is available to fall back on, rather than a sanitized message.

## Evidence
`vite.config.js:1747-1764`:
```js
const stale = cache;
const request = coalesceProxyRequest(inFlight, 'recent-launches', refreshUpstream);
try {
  const fresh = await request.promise;
  send(res, 200, fresh.body, request.shared ? 'INFLIGHT' : 'MISS');
} catch (error) {
  if (stale) {
    ...
    send(res, 200, stale.body, 'STALE-ERROR');
    return;
  }
  send(
    res,
    Number.isInteger(error?.upstreamStatus) ? error.upstreamStatus : 502,
    error?.upstreamBody || JSON.stringify({ error: 'Launch Library 2 unavailable' }),
    'NONE',
  );
}
```
`error.upstreamBody` is set directly from the upstream response body in `refreshUpstream()` (`vite.config.js:1720-1726`) and relayed to the client unmodified when present. No API key is exposed this way (`LL2_API_TOKEN` is only ever attached via the `Authorization` header in `launchLibraryRequestHeaders()`, `vite.config.js:1657-1664`, never placed in the URL or body), but this breaks the sanitized-error convention this same file documents elsewhere (e.g. `tomtomProxy`'s comment at `vite.config.js:1904-1905`) and contradicts the "error payloads are sanitized" closure note in `docs/KNOWN-ISSUES.md` ("Proxy SSRF and error-surface hardening gaps", closed as fixed).

## Why this matters
Relaying an unsanitized third-party error body to arbitrary clients is a minor information-disclosure inconsistency: it can leak upstream infrastructure details, unexpected formats, or verbose error text that a generic message would otherwise suppress, and it's inconsistent with how the rest of the proxy layer is documented to behave.

## Attack or failure scenario
Launch Library 2 returns an unusual error (rate limit message, maintenance banner, malformed request diagnostic, etc.) with no prior successful cache; that body is relayed to the client as-is instead of being replaced with a generic message.

## Root cause
This one route's error path was written before (or missed) the sanitized-error convention applied elsewhere in the file.

## Recommended fix
Replace `error?.upstreamBody` in the client-facing response with a generic message (matching the pattern used by other proxies in this file, e.g. `trackBackfillProxies`'s `Track source HTTP ${upstream.status}`), and log the raw upstream body server-side only if needed for debugging.

## Acceptance criteria
- On upstream failure with no stale cache, the client receives a generic error message, not the raw upstream body.

## Suggested labels
bug

## Priority
P3

## Severity
Low — no secret exposure, but a real deviation from the project's own stated and largely-implemented error-sanitization convention.

## Confidence
Confirmed — read the full failure path in `rocketLaunchesProxy()` and the header-construction function that proves no token reaches this path.

