## TL;DR

When the same successful GEV action sequence repeats in the same bounded context, it should be possible to turn it into a deterministic shortcut over time:

```
repeated explicit workflow
  -> candidate
  -> suggestion
  -> user-confirmed macro
  -> reusable reflex
  -> drift demotion
```

This is not hidden UI automation and not permission for a model to click around.

The compiled routine is a sequence of existing typed GEV actions whose outcomes are still verified by their owners.

## Why now

GEV increasingly has a shared semantic action vocabulary:

- voice tools;
- #813 command-palette proposal;
- selected-entity evidence actions (#797);
- panel/layer/context controls;
- scene/camera actions.

That makes repeated workflows identifiable without recording raw pointer/keyboard replay.

Examples:
- select fire -> Recent Imagery -> nearby CCTV;
- enable Weather -> Radar -> Tactical HUD;
- select aircraft -> Contacts -> Cockpit.

## Smallest useful slices

### Slice 1 — action receipt format

Define a bounded plain-data episode:

```js
{
  contextFingerprint,
  actions: [
    { name, args, result }
  ],
  correctedOrUndone,
  startedAt,
  completedAt
}
```

Development-only capture. No routine learning yet.

### Slice 2 — deterministic routine candidate detector

Given fixtures, detect only exact/stable repeated action-name + normalized-arg sequences.

Output a candidate with count and success rate.

No model call.

### Slice 3 — suggestion only

For one reversible workflow, surface:
“Save this as a shortcut?”

The user explicitly names/accepts it.

### Slice 4 — confirmed macro

Execute the stored typed sequence through the same action runner, with normal cancellation and result verification.

### Slice 5 — drift demotion

Disable/demote the routine when:
- an action/schema disappears;
- required capability/source is unavailable;
- verified outcomes start failing;
- repeated user undo/override occurs.

## Authority rules

- a routine stores only authority explicitly granted to that routine;
- preparation/suggestion does not imply commit permission;
- a routine never inherits a sensitive permission from another workflow;
- no raw DOM selectors or coordinate clicks;
- verification comes from action results, not the macro definition.

