## Summary

| Issue | Severity | Reach |
|-------|----------|-------|
| `/api/terrain/heights` has **no cross-site gate**, accepts **any finite coordinate**, and keeps **every point it ever resolves** in a cache that is **never evicted** and is **re-serialized to disk on every 15 s flush** | Medium: resource exhaustion (memory, disk, CPU) and abuse of the free Re:Earth service from the user's IP. No data disclosure and no spend of a keyed provider | Any web page open in the same browser while the app runs (an `<img>` tag is enough), or any LAN client under `HOST=0.0.0.0` |

This is a separate fix from #922 (CCTV Street View spend and the `.gev-logs`/`.gev-cache` file exposure). It does not overlap with that PR.

## Root cause

`server/providers/terrain.js` (before):

```js
server.middlewares.use('/api/terrain/heights', async (req, res) => {   // no same-site gate
  ...
  const points = parseTerrainPoints(rawPoints);   // any finite number: 1e9,1e9 is accepted
  if (points.length > MAX_POINTS) { send(500, ...) }  // MAX_POINTS = 2000
  const outcome = await resolveTerrainHeightRequest({
    points,
    cache: mem,            // const mem = new Map(): no size cap, no eviction, 30-day TTL
    fetchMissing: fetchMissingSingleFlight,   // 64-point upstream chunks, up to 4 attempts each
    ttlMs: TTL_MS,
  });
```

```js
// every 15 s while dirty: the whole map, every time
const obj = { version: 2, points: Object.fromEntries(mem.entries()) };
await fsp.writeFile(CACHE_PATH, JSON.stringify(obj), 'utf8');
```

Each request may name 2,000 never-seen points. Each one becomes an upstream lookup (2000 / 64 = 32 sequential calls to `terrain.reearth.land`) and a permanent `mem` entry. The full map is then rewritten to `.gev-cache/terrain-heights.json` on every flush. Nothing ever removes an entry: expired points are only refreshed, never deleted. As a result, memory, flush CPU and the cache file all grow linearly with the number of distinct points anyone sends.

The cost-bearing routes (`/api/google/*`, `/api/openai/*`, `/api/realtime/*`) already refuse cross-site browser requests through `admitSameSite`. This route was left open, so the caller does not have to be on the machine. A cross-site `<img src="http://localhost:5173/api/terrain/heights?points=...">` sends no `Origin`, only `Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site`, and it was answered.

The client already bounds its own copy of this data (`src/services/terrainHeights.js`, `CACHE_MAX_ENTRIES = 20_000`, with a comment explaining why an unbounded cache is a problem). The server cache did not get the same treatment.

