### What happened

The Overpass response cache is keyed only by the query, not by which upstream produced the
answer. So after an operator changes `OVERPASS_UPSTREAMS`, the app can keep serving responses
fetched from the *previous* upstream — for 7 days (`OVERPASS_DISK_TTL_MS`), 30 days for
boundary-class queries (`OVERPASS_BOUNDARY_DISK_TTL_MS`), and indefinitely through the
serve-stale path, which reads with `Infinity`.

This matters most for the path #742 created. The closing note on #648 says Overpass is now used
"only when someone configures their own instance" — but an install that ran **before** #742 has
a `.gev-cache/overpass/` populated from the then-default public instances
(`overpass-api.de`, `overpass.kumi.systems`, `lz4.overpass-api.de`,
`overpass.private.coffee` — pre-#742 `constants.js:20-29`). The disk path function and the
accepted on-disk shape are unchanged across #742, so those files are still read and served
today. Configuring your own instance does not displace them, and the operator gets no signal
that their instance is not being used.

A second, upstream-independent consequence: switching between two instances with different data
vintages silently keeps the older one.

### Steps to reproduce

Two separate processes so only the **disk** layer can carry the value, starting from an empty
`.gev-cache/overpass/`. Phase 1 points `OVERPASS_UPSTREAMS` at a local stand-in that returns a
marked payload; phase 2 points it at a different, real Overpass instance and repeats the same
query.

```js
