### Mechanism

- `overpass.js:128` — `cacheKey = safeBody.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim()`; the sanitized request
  body is the whole key.
- `overpass/cache.js:25-29` — `overpassDiskPath(cacheKey)` is
  `sha1(cacheKey) + '.json'`. The endpoint is not part of the path or the key.
- `overpass/cache.js:113-118` — `readStaleOverpass` reads with `Infinity`, so a past-TTL entry
  from any previous upstream is still served whenever the current one fails.

The information needed to invalidate is already in the files: pre-#742 payloads stored the real
endpoint (`transport.js:112` of that revision), and `docs/CURRENT-STATE.md:3656` notes "old
cached endpoint metadata". That metadata is kept out of responses and errors, but never compared
on read. Current payloads store `endpoint: 'configured'` (`overpass/transport.js:189`), which
also cannot distinguish two configured instances from each other.

**The same shape appears a second time** in the installations cache:
`military-installations/cache.js:93-102` keys by `sha1(cacheKey)` where the key is only the
bbox (`military-installations.js:112-114`), with a 30-day disk TTL and two `Infinity` stale
reads (`military-installations.js:119`, `:195`). It draws its endpoints from the same
`resolveOverpassUpstreams`.

### Suggested fix

The repo already has a convention for operator-set configuration, in
`server/providers/local-receivers.js`: a rejected `LOCAL_RECEIVER_FEEDS` entry is logged with
the variable name, the specific reason, and the consequence (`:416-420`), and surfaces as
`status: 'invalid'` on the product surface (`:356`). Running it:

```
[local-receivers] LOCAL_RECEIVER_FEEDS feed-1 (1090 MHz) rejected:
                  host must be loopback, RFC1918, localhost or *.local. It will not be fetched.
```

Applying the same idea here would be either of:

1. Include an endpoint-set fingerprint in the cache key (a hash of the resolved upstream list),
   so changing `OVERPASS_UPSTREAMS` naturally misses and refetches. Old files then age out
   untouched instead of being served.
2. Keep the key and store the fingerprint in the payload, rejecting on read when it differs —
   closer to what the old `endpoint` field was already recording.

Either way, a one-line startup log naming how many cached entries were produced by a different
upstream would make the current silent case visible.

