﻿## Summary

| | |
|---|---|
| **What** | `/api/tomtom/flow/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` and `/api/tomtom/status` are the only keyed, billed proxy routes still outside the cross-site gate from #900 (Mapillary, OpenAI and Places are gated). |
| **Who can trigger it** | Any web page the user opens while God's Eye View is running with `TOMTOM_API_KEY` set. A plain `<img>` tag is enough: no CORS, no user interaction, and the response never needs to be readable. |
| **Impact** | The page spends the user's TomTom quota and switches the traffic layer off (`429 {"error":"budget"}` for every tile not already cached) until UTC midnight. Repeating it each day uses up 6,000 of the 200,000-per-month free allowance per day. |
| **Severity (honest)** | **Medium**: cost and availability only. The key itself is never exposed, since it stays server-side and responses are opaque cross-site. |
| **Fix** | Add `if (admitSameSite(req, res)) return;` at the top of the handler, the same line the Places routes use. |

## Root cause

`server/providers/traffic.js` counts every uncached tile as a billed attempt against the persistent daily budget (`.gev-cache/tomtom/budget.json`):

```js
async function fetchUpstream(z, x, y) {
  ...
  recordUpstreamFetch(); // attempts count — upstream bills the request either way
```

The middleware runs this for any request with valid coordinates. `isValidTileCoord` accepts z8 through z16, which is about 5.7 billion distinct tiles, so cache hits never protect the budget. There is no Origin, `Sec-Fetch-Site` or proxy-header check:

```js
server.middlewares.use('/api/tomtom', async (req, res) => {
  // no admitSameSite(...) here
```

A hostile page only needs this:

```html
<script>
  for (let x = 0; x < 6000; x++) {
    new Image().src = `http://localhost:5173/api/tomtom/flow/16/${30000 + x}/20000.pbf`;
  }
</script>
```

Chromium sends these with `Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site` and no `Origin`. That is exactly the case `admitSameSiteRequest` rule (2) exists to refuse; this route just never calls it.

