### Summary

Street Traffic, Military Installations and the voice outline source read road and military
geometry from `tiles.openfreemap.org`. A self-hoster who runs their own OpenFreeMap-compatible
tile server cannot point the app at it without editing source. No environment variable, settings
field or launcher option reaches the tile source.

#935 (for #933) fixed the code seam underneath. `createVectorTileSource` now derives
`allowedOrigin` from `tileJsonUrl`, so a caller that repoints only `tileJsonUrl` gets tiles from
that host or a named error. That seam works, as measured below. What is missing is a way for an
operator to use it.

Compare `OVERPASS_UPSTREAMS`, `NOMINATIM_URL` and `PHOTON_URL`, which already let an operator
replace a public service with their own.

### Where the endpoint is fixed

- `src/sources/openFreeMap.js:366` sets `tileJsonUrl: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/planet'`.
- `src/layers/traffic/source.js:98` and `src/layers/installations/source.js:94` call
  `createOpenFreeMapSource()` as a default parameter, with no endpoint.
- `src/sources/openFreeMapOutlines.js:289` does the same for outlines.
- `.env.example` has no tile variable. `import.meta.env` in `src/` reads only
  `GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY`, `CESIUM_ION_TOKEN`, `PHOTON_URL` and `DEV`.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Check out `upstream/main` (measured at `9bca22834`) and run `npm ci` on Node 24.
2. Start a small caching mirror of OpenFreeMap. `/planet` returns the upstream TileJSON
   unchanged, the way a transparent `proxy_cache` would. `/local/planet` rewrites `tiles[0]` onto
   the mirror's own origin.

   ```js
   // tile-mirror.mjs
   import http from 'node:http';
   const PORT = 12348, UP = 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org', cache = new Map();
   http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
     const local = req.url.startsWith('/local/');
     const path = local ? req.url.slice('/local'.length) : req.url;
     if (!cache.has(path)) {
       const r = await fetch(UP + path);
       cache.set(path, { status: r.status, type: r.headers.get('content-type'), body: Buffer.from(await r.arrayBuffer()) });
     }
     const e = cache.get(path);
     let body = e.body;
     if (local && path === '/planet') {
       const j = JSON.parse(body.toString());
       j.tiles = j.tiles.map((t) => t.replace(UP, `http://127.0.0.1:${PORT}`));
       body = Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(j));
     }
     res.writeHead(e.status, { 'content-type': e.type || 'application/octet-stream' }).end(body);
   }).listen(PORT, '127.0.0.1');
   ```

3. From the repository root, drive the production factory against it:

   ```js
   // tile-probe.mjs
   import { createOpenFreeMapSource } from './src/sources/openFreeMap.js';
   const MIRROR = 'http://127.0.0.1:12348';
   for (const [name, opts] of [
     ['A default', {}],
     ['B tileJsonUrl=mirror, TileJSON verbatim', { tileJsonUrl: `${MIRROR}/planet` }],
     ['C tileJsonUrl=mirror, mirror serves its own TileJSON', { tileJsonUrl: `${MIRROR}/local/planet` }],
   ]) {
     const source = createOpenFreeMapSource(opts);
     let template = null, error = null;
     try { template = (await source.getMetadata()).template; }
     catch (e) { error = `${e.message} | cause: ${e.cause?.message}`; }
     if (!error) await source.fetchBounds({ west: 7.4, south: 43.72, east: 7.44, north: 43.75 }, { zoom: 14 });
     console.log(name, '\n  template:', template, '\n  error:', error);
   }
   ```

### Measured output

```
A default
  template: https://tiles.openfreemap.org/planet/20261101_080013_pt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
  error: null
B tileJsonUrl=mirror, TileJSON verbatim
  template: null
  error: Vector tile metadata unavailable | cause: Invalid vector tile origin
C tileJsonUrl=mirror, mirror serves its own TileJSON
  template: http://127.0.0.1:12348/planet/20261101_080013_pt/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
  error: null
```

In case C, `fetchBounds` completed without error and every tile request went to the mirror (a
logging variant of the mirror recorded the tile requests arriving there). Case B fails with a named error instead of silently reading from upstream, which is
the behavior #935 introduced. So the code path is ready. Only the wiring is missing.

### Browser-side note

The page's `connect-src` is `'self' blob: data: https: wss: ws:` (`build/vite.js:77`). A tile
server on plain `http://` on another origin is blocked in the browser even when the code accepts
it. An operator needs either an `https` tile host or a same-origin path behind the app's own
reverse proxy. That deserves one line in the docs next to any new variable.

### Proposed direction

Add one optional variable, for example `OPENFREEMAP_TILEJSON_URL`, read once and passed as
`tileJsonUrl` to the three `createOpenFreeMapSource` call sites. Unset keeps today's default.
The origin check stays as it is, so a TileJSON still cannot send the client to a host the operator
did not configure. Document that the TileJSON's `tiles[0]` must be on the same origin as the
TileJSON URL (case C), or offer a `template` variable that skips TileJSON entirely.

